# ${{NAME}} - finish the event signups your agent handed off

When your OpenClaw agent hits something only you can do on an event form (a
captcha, a sign-in, a question your profile doesn't answer), it queues the event.
This opens each queued event in YOUR Chrome through Codex browser use (the ChatGPT
Chrome extension), fills in everything the agent already knew, and stops for the
human-only steps. It **never submits**.

A record can be any registration page - Luma, a hackathon portal, a waitlist, a
survey. Adapt to the page: steps 3b-3d only apply when the page has that thing.

**Hyphens only, never em-dashes, in anything you type into a form.**

The queue commands below write to the queue folder (and push it, when the queue is
shared through git). If the sandbox blocks one, ask the user to approve running it
rather than skipping it.

## Steps

1. **Read the queue.** It syncs first when the queue is shared through git.

   ```sh
   bash "{{SIGNUPQ}}" list
   ```

   If it prints `[]`, say "No pending signups." and stop.

2. **Connect to Chrome:** use browser use with the `chrome` backend - the user's
   own Chrome, where they are signed in. Not the in-app browser, not Computer Use.
   Read its documentation first, as the browser skill requires. If Chrome is not
   available, say "Chrome isn't connected - open Chrome, check the ChatGPT
   extension is installed and signed in, then run ${{NAME}} again." and stop. Do
   not guess, and do not fall back to another browser.

3. **One record at a time**, oldest first. For each:

   a. Open a NEW tab, go to its `url`, and wait for the page to load. Codex may
      ask the user to allow the site first. If that is declined, say so and stop -
      do not retry or work around it.

   b. **Sign in - only if the page needs it.** If a sign-in wall stands where the
      form should be, ask the user to sign in in that tab, then stop and wait for
      them. Never type a password. Plain forms (waitlists, surveys) need no
      sign-in: skip this.

   c. **Already registered?** If the page shows a registered state ("You're in",
      "Going", "Registered", "On the waitlist", "Pending approval", "Thanks for
      registering"), close the record (step 4, note "already registered") and go
      to the next one. Never register twice.

   d. **Reveal the form** if it sits behind a Register / RSVP / Request to join /
      Get ticket / Apply button: click it, and pick the record's `ticket` if the
      page offers a choice. If the fields are already on the page, go straight to
      filling.

   e. **Fill every `prefill` field**, matching each key to its form field, and
      check each one before moving on (read the value back, or take a screenshot
      for custom widgets):
      - **Text / textarea:** fill it in one action (the documented fill call on
        the field's locator), then read the value back. Do not type long text key
        by key - it drops characters.
      - **Native `<select>`:** select the exact option label (e.g. Country ->
        "Germany").
      - **Searchable dropdown** (typing filters the list): type the value to
        filter, click the option, check it.
      - **Custom dropdown** (a fixed option panel, typing does not filter):
        (1) click the field to open it; (2) take a screenshot; (3) click the option
        from THAT screenshot; (4) click an empty area to close the panel - it
        lingers and hijacks your next click otherwise; (5) screenshot and confirm
        the field shows your value. One dropdown at a time.
      - **Phone with a country selector:** set the country code if it is not
        already right, then the number.
      - **Required checkboxes:** tick what the form requires to submit (code of
        conduct, privacy, terms, "None" for dietary). Do not opt in to marketing
        or extras unless a `prefill` value says so.
      - If you cannot confidently match a value to a field, leave it and add it to
        the list for the user. Never guess personal facts.

   f. **Stop before submitting.** Never click the final submit button and never
      attempt a captcha. In one short message tell the user what is left: each
      `needs_user` item, anything you could not fill, then the human check
      (captcha) if any and the submit button. Leave the tab open on the form.

   g. **Close the record** (step 4) with the note "prefilled, left for the user
      to submit", then move on. If the user never finishes it, sending the link
      to their agent again puts it back in the queue.

4. **Closing a record:**

   ```sh
   bash "{{SIGNUPQ}}" done --id <id> --notes "<short note>"
   ```

   Use `--status skipped` instead if the user says they don't want the event.

5. **At the end**, list each event in one line: its title, then what is left
   for the user in its tab.
